<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Brown</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Brown was an American abolitionist and evangelical Christian who believed he was an instrument of God raised to strike the death blow to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, the leading exponent of violence in the abolitionist movement. During the Bleeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a> crisis he and his sons killed five supporters of slavery in the Pottawatomie massacre of May 1856. In October 1859 he led a raid on the federal armory at Harpers Ferry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, meaning to arm enslaved people, but few joined and his men were killed or captured by militia and US Marines under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>. Hanged for treason in December 1859, Brown was viewed as a hero in the North, and the raid escalated the tensions that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-brown-abolitionist.jpg" alt="John Brown" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Augustus Washington · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1846-47_John_Brown_by_Augustus_Washington_(without_frame)_(Restoration).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
